--TEST--
`rule:types/decimal` and `rule:types/arithmetic`: `Core\Decimal::pow` throws at both of the type's bounds rather than narrowing the scale or wrapping the mantissa
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The scale bound, which a base with fractional digits of its own reaches
// fast: `1.05` carries two, so the fifteenth power wants thirty and the type
// has twenty-eight. Narrowing to a scale that fits would make the power a
// second inexact operation without saying so.
decimal $rate = 1.05;
try {
    echo Core\Decimal::pow($rate, 15), "\n";
} catch (ArithmeticError $deep) {
    echo $deep->message, "\n";
}

// The mantissa bound, from the side with no fractional digit at all: 10^28
// fits in 96 bits and 10^29 does not.
decimal $ten = 10;
echo Core\Decimal::pow($ten, 28), "\n";
try {
    echo Core\Decimal::pow($ten, 29), "\n";
} catch (ArithmeticError $wide) {
    echo $wide->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Decimal::pow cannot answer 1.05 to the power 15: a `decimal` holds 28 fractional digits and a 96-bit mantissa
10000000000000000000000000000
Core\Decimal::pow cannot answer 10 to the power 29: a `decimal` holds 28 fractional digits and a 96-bit mantissa
